1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:02,080
Hey, everyone here.

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:04,800
We'll be taking a look at Stay Even Analysis.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,280
What it is and why you might use it.

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:11,800
Let's say you're thinking about lowering your price to boost sales.

5
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:12,880
It feels intuitive.

6
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:16,000
Drop the price, attract more customers, make more money.

7
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:18,640
But here's what's easy to miss.

8
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:23,040
If you cut your price, you're giving up profit per unit.

9
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:28,880
That means to earn the same total profit, you'll need to sell more units, sometimes a lot more.

10
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:37,720
In this case, we've lowered our price from $10 to $9, and we need to sell an additional 10 units just to make up that shortfall.

11
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:41,400
So the real question isn't will I sell more?

12
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:45,640
It's how much more do I have to sell just to break even?

13
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:48,760
That's the logic behind Stay Even Analysis.

14
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:57,040
It allows you to determine the quantity required to offset a change in cost, price, or or other revenue factors.

15
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:02,520
It's a simple but powerful tool to test whether a price change makes financial sense.

16
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:08,360
Let's say you sell a Software subscription for $100 per month.

17
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:19,880
Your marginal cost, that is what it costs to serve one more customer, is $40, and your contribution margin is 100 minus 40 divided by 100 or 60%.

18
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:28,440
Now, suppose you're considering a 10% price cut from $100 to $90 just to stay competitive.

19
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:30,920
You'll make less per customer.

20
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:34,040
But if more people sign up, you could still come out ahead.

21
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,560
Perhaps.  Let's run the numbers.

22
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:39,720
The Stay Even formula that we're going to use is Stay even quantity percent.

23
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:43,640
That is how much we need to maintain where we were.

24
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:49,920
That's equal to the price change percent or the 10% that we saw earlier divided by our contribution margin.

25
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,480
In this case, it was 60%.

26
00:01:52,560 --> 00:02:00,960
So this gives us a state even quantity of 10% divided by 60%, or approximately 17%.

27
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:13,760
So to make the same profit after cutting the price, you'll need to increase your sales by at least 17% or a 17% volume increase.

28
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:16,560
Sounds abstract, but if you sell 1,000 more.

29
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:28,520
If you sell 1,000 units per month, that's 170 extra units you have to sell or generate 15, 300 in new revenue at $90.

30
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:40,760
So turning that percentage into a concrete sales target helps teams internalize the challenge of can our campaign really deliver 170 extra units this month?

31
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,760
If not, we shouldn't cut the price.

32
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:44,680
If you can do it, then that's great.

33
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:50,880
But if demand isn't responsive enough, you may end up working harder and getting less in return.

34
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:54,080
Now, a note on the formula that we Just used.

35
00:02:54,240 --> 00:03:02,160
There are two common formulas that we can use and one and they're both suited to different business contexts and have different assumptions.

36
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:07,800
The first formula here is used in economics, pricing strategy and marginal analysis.

37
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:14,920
This version assumes a direct trade off between profit loss per per unit and the volume of units sold.

38
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:20,080
So it's making it's well suited for long run pricing models or strategic planning.

39
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:27,120
The output tells you how much quantity must increase to offset the loss in unit profit.

40
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:31,680
This is useful when considering price adjustments in a broader business model.

41
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:40,240
The second formula, the one above here, the one that's used in the text, is more common in retail marketing and promotional pricing analysis.

42
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:44,080
It's especially useful for short term discounting decisions.

43
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:52,800
This formula adjusts for the price lost relative to the full price, making it more accurate in scenarios like flash sales or seasonal promotions.

44
00:03:53,040 --> 00:04:03,760
The output represents an adjusted quantity increase needed to stay even, assuming both price and contribution are working from a percentage base.

45
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:13,240
In summary, the first formula is better for strategic long term pricing decisions, while the second is ideal for tactical short term promotional campaigns.

46
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:15,800
Now let's take it a step further.

47
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:22,600
Let's use state even analysis with historical sales and then do a few reverse engineering calculations.

48
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:26,440
Let's say that we have three different pricing campaigns.

49
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:28,360
We're going to run different discounts in these.

50
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:30,200
Here we're going to have our structure.

51
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:33,160
Well, first let's clarify one thing.

52
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:44,120
In the first scenario, we're going to assume that our contribution margin is held constant, just to simplify the comparison and isolate the effect of the price discount and sales volume lift.

53
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,320
So we have our three campaigns.

54
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:48,920
We have our original price of $100 across all three.

55
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:56,040
We have three different discounts, 10%, 15% and 5%, which gives us new prices of 90, 85 and $95.

56
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:00,880
And we kept our contribution margin the same at 60%.

57
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:08,800
Now what we want to do is calculate the stay even quantity percentage for each of these different campaigns.

58
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:10,400
And we're going to do that right here.

59
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:12,160
So we got 16.7%.

60
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:13,240
We've already seen this.

61
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:15,600
We divided the 10% by the 60%.

62
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Now we're going to do campaign B and C, divide the 15% by 60% to get 25% and the 5% by 60% to get the 8.33%.

63
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:36,560
Now the actual volume gain, what actually took place when we ran these campaigns, now these are made up numbers, but you would actually do this in a real world scenario.

64
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:51,200
The actual volume gain was 20% for Campaign A, 18% for Campaign B, 12% for Campaign C and what we see is that campaign A and C were profitable with their discounts of 10 and 5% respectively.

65
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:58,280
But campaign B wasn't because the stay even volume gain percentage that we needed was 25%.

66
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:02,920
But our actual volume gain was only 18%.

67
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:06,440
So that was a loss making result and one that we tried to avoid.

68
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:11,240
Now, now let's take a look at what happens when we allow our contribution margin to vary.

69
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:15,280
So here we're going to have our new contribution margins.

70
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:17,800
The prices and discounts and all those things were the same.

71
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:25,600
But our new contribution margins do change from 60% to 55.6, 53 and 58%.

72
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:33,520
Okay, so now we take those contribution margins and we take the original discounts to figure out what our stay even volume gain percentage is.

73
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:39,760
And now we have 18%, 28.3% and 8.6%.

74
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:50,360
So what again we notice is that campaign A and campaign C were successful because they were still profitable, but campaign B was not.

75
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:52,920
It was still a loss making campaign.

76
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:58,840
So what we see is that we underperform at discounts of 15% or greater.

77
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:09,000
So in this case, we could use this information going forward to help us figure out what the price we should set just to make sure that we still remain profitable.

78
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:16,240
Now let's look at what happens when we're curious about reverse engineering some price cuts.

79
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:26,320
So we want to know if I can only realistically increase volume by 10%, how much can I lower price without losing profit?

80
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:34,960
So we're going to set up the formula to be the max price drop percent that we can have is equal to the expected volume gain percent.

81
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:39,400
In this case, 10% right here times our contribution margin.

