85
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:05,680
Now we can also build a reverse calculator here.

86
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:07,840
We could use this for managers.

87
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:12,200
In this case, it's going to give us a realistic forecasted volume gain.

88
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:14,680
It's also going to give us a product contribution margin.

89
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:18,360
And it looks something like this expected volume gain.

90
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:21,240
Now these are what we would expect built off of past experience.

91
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:30,840
5, 10, 15 and 20% the different contribution margins and you could adjust these based off of different things and then max allowable discount.

92
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:33,000
So let's look at this table.

93
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:42,400
If we think sales will rise by 5%, then we can afford to offer up up to a 3% discount only on any price.

94
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:46,080
Now any more than 3% and you're going to lose profit.

95
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:52,160
So unless your sales increase by more than 5%, you don't want to offer more than a 3% discount.

96
00:08:52,640 --> 00:09:01,200
A 10% expected sales lift, that is the expected volume gain percentage there gives you room to offer up to 6% discount.

97
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:02,920
That's our break even limit there.

98
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:08,920
Price cuts above this level require bigger sales jumps to justify it.

99
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:22,400
15% sales increase can justify it to a 9% discount and then a 20% expected volume gain percentage can justify up to a 12% price cut or discount.

100
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:31,320
So this decision support tool here helps us or tells us if we only expect a month modest volume boost, we can't afford a big discount.

101
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:37,240
Lower our contribution margin is, the more limited our discounting options become.

102
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:39,000
And vice versa.

103
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:41,160
So why does this matter?

104
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:45,200
This is a tool for smarter pricing here.  Stay even.

105
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:47,560
Analysis doesn't tell us what price to choose.

106
00:09:47,800 --> 00:10:02,520
It tells us what needs to happen after a price change is especially helpful when we're running discounts or temporary promotions, entering new markets and testing pricing strategies, or if we're facing pressure to match competitors.

107
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:05,000
It also ties back to elasticity.

108
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:15,080
If our product has elastic demand, you may be able to increase sales by 20% or more, making the discount worthwhile so you can adjust your prices down.

109
00:10:15,320 --> 00:10:23,480
But if you have inelastic demand, adjusting prices down may cause you to lose more revenue than what was justified.  So stay even.

110
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:24,920
Analysis is a reality check.

111
00:10:24,920 --> 00:10:28,960
For us, it's the difference between guessing and planning.

112
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:33,800
Because pricing isn't just about how much you sell, it's about how much you keep.  And that's it.

